                      (   unit_tests.asm):09069         ; TSTCOMPWORDS - compiling-words tests (glossary section 3.10,
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09070         ; 14 words, 19 tests since several get separate cases: TICK
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09071         ; found/not-found, ['] compiling/interpreting state, POSTPONE
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09072         ; normal/immediate word, [COMPILE] normal/immediate word,
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09073         ; SLITERAL compiling/interpreting state, ABORT" false/true flag).
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09074         ; Reuses the scratch infrastructure established for sections
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09075         ; 3.8/3.9 throughout.
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09076         ;
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09077         ; POSTPONE is this section's hardest case: a genuine two-level
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09078         ; compile-time mechanism. Traced its own code first - for a
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09079         ; normal word, it compiles code that ITSELF compiles a call to
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09080         ; that word later, when the current definition eventually runs,
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09081         ; not immediately (standard ANS semantics: POSTPONE appends a
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09082         ; word's own compilation semantics to the current definition,
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09083         ; and for an ordinary word that semantics IS "compile a call to
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09084         ; it", so appending it only makes sense if the current
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09085         ; definition later performs that compiling action itself,
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09086         ; against whatever's being compiled at that later point).
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09087         ; Verified via the compiled byte sequence directly rather than
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09088         ; fully executing a triple-nested compile chain.
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09089         ;
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09090         ; [COMPILE] is POSTPONE's obsolescent predecessor (added for ANS
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09091         ; Annex F test coverage, F.6.2.2530) - simpler to verify since it
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09092         ; makes no normal/immediate distinction at all: both its test
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09093         ; cases (TSTXCOMPILE1/2) check for the exact same direct-call
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09094         ; byte sequence POSTPONE only produces for the immediate case
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09095         ; (TSTPOSTPONE2) - see XCOMPILE's own header/comment for why.
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09096         ;
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09097         ; ABORT" is safely testable for both its flag cases - confirmed
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09098         ; by reading its own runtime code that it uses THROW -2
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09099         ; internally, not the raw, never-returns ABORT mechanism plain
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09100         ; ABORT itself uses (still deliberately untested, per section
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09101         ; 3.1's own reasoning).
                      (   unit_tests.asm):09102         ; ------------------------------------------------------------
