                      (   unit_tests.asm):12636         ; TSTDOTQUOTE - unit test for .". No STATE check at all in its
                      (   unit_tests.asm):12637         ; own code (confirmed by reading it directly - matches "no
                      (   unit_tests.asm):12638         ; interpretation semantics, per ANS" without a specific -14
                      (   unit_tests.asm):12639         ; throw, unlike [CHAR]/S"), so only the compiling case is
                      (   unit_tests.asm):12640         ; tested, matching how it's actually meant to be used. Its own
                      (   unit_tests.asm):12641         ; runtime (DOTSTR) calls TYPE internally, which has two entirely
                      (   unit_tests.asm):12642         ; different implementations gated by SERIALPOLL (the same real
                      (   unit_tests.asm):12643         ; finding from section 3.1) - applying that lesson from the
                      (   unit_tests.asm):12644         ; start here rather than re-discovering it: EMITCH works
                      (   unit_tests.asm):12645         ; universally for a single-character check, but this outputs
                      (   unit_tests.asm):12646         ; multiple characters, so - matching TSTCR's own established
                      (   unit_tests.asm):12647         ; fix - the full OUTHEAD/OUTBUF check only applies under
                      (   unit_tests.asm):12648         ; SERIALPOLL=0, with a narrower EMITCH-based check (last
                      (   unit_tests.asm):12649         ; character only, plus the stack-depth check) for SERIALPOLL=1.
                      (   unit_tests.asm):12650         ; ------------------------------------------------------------
