                      (   unit_tests.asm):14969         ; TSTEXCEPTION - exception handling tests (glossary section
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14970         ; 3.15, 2 words, 4 tests since CATCH/THROW can only be
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14971         ; meaningfully tested together - THROW's own effect is only
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14972         ; observable through a CATCH that traps it). Already used
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14973         ; extensively as this whole session's own error-testing
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14974         ; mechanism throughout sections 3.8-3.14, so their correctness
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14975         ; has substantial indirect evidence already; this section still
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14976         ; gets its own, direct, dedicated tests.
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14977         ;
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14978         ; Critical safety constraint, confirmed by reading THROW's own
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14979         ; code directly: with no active CATCH, THROW jumps straight to
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14980         ; ABORT (unsafe to trigger directly - it resets the return
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14981         ; stack, destroying this whole test framework's own call chain,
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14982         ; per section 3.1's own established reasoning). Every THROW in
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14983         ; this section's own tests is either wrapped in a genuine,
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14984         ; active CATCH (via a dedicated internal helper, TSTTHROWHLP,
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14985         ; that only ever gets invoked that way), or is a THROW(0) call -
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14986         ; confirmed via THROW's own code to be a pure, unconditional
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14987         ; no-op that never touches HANDLER or ABORT at all, so that one
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14988         ; specific case is safe to call directly.
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14989         ;
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14990         ; TSTCATCHTHROW specifically verifies the two dummy values
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14991         ; TSTTHROWHLP pushes before throwing are genuinely gone
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14992         ; afterward, confirming CATCH's own documented "restores data
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14993         ; stack depth... on either path" for real. TSTHANDLERSAVE
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14994         ; verifies the HANDLER half of that same documented guarantee
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14995         ; directly, by reading HANDLER itself before and after CATCH
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14996         ; calls on both the success and throw paths, rather than only
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14997         ; inferring it from stack-level behavior.
                      (   unit_tests.asm):14998         ; ------------------------------------------------------------
